## Common Network Attacks

### ARP Spoofing / Man-in-the-Middle (MitM)
Attacker poisons ARP cache so victim traffic flows through their machine. Enables interception, credential theft, SSL stripping.  
**Defend:** Dynamic ARP Inspection (DAI), static ARP entries, encrypted protocols.

### DDoS / DoS
Flood target with traffic to exhaust resources. Variants: UDP flood, ICMP flood, SYN flood. Amplification attacks abuse misconfigured DNS/NTP servers to multiply traffic 10–50×.

### Evil Twin / Rogue AP
Attacker sets up a Wi-Fi hotspot with a legitimate-looking SSID. All client traffic is intercepted.  
**Defend:** VPN on public Wi-Fi, verify certificates.

### Packet Sniffing
Capture network traffic with Wireshark or tcpdump. On unencrypted protocols (HTTP, FTP, Telnet) credentials are visible in plaintext.  
**Defend:** Use TLS/SSH everywhere. No cleartext protocols.

### Port Scanning & Reconnaissance
Attacker maps open ports and running services before exploitation. Tools: `nmap`, `masscan`.  
**Defend:** IDS/IPS, firewall logging, restrict unnecessary open ports.

